<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ishiyama Hongan-ji</h1><p class="st-meta">1533 CE – 1580 CE · capital Ishiyama (Osaka)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The fortified headquarters of the True Pure Land Buddhist sect at Osaka, which withstood Oda Nobunaga for ten years before surrendering in 1580.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After its temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamashina-hongan-ji">Yamashina</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> was burned by rival forces in 1532, the leadership of the Hongan-ji moved in 1533 to Ishiyama, a site on high ground at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-river">Yodo</a> river, where a temple had been founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the head priests Shonyo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennyo">Kennyo</a>, Ishiyama became a moated fortress and temple town, supported by donations from believers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko leagues</a> that controlled provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga</a>. The abbot acted as a great lord, negotiating with warlords and the court, and the surrounding town grew into a commercial centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1570 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennyo">Kennyo</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, supplied by sea by the Mori navy until Nobunaga's iron-clad ships cut the route in 1578. Mediated by the emperor, Kennyo surrendered in 1580. Hideyoshi built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> on the site, and the Hongan-ji later split into two branches.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ishiyama-hongan-ji.jpg" alt="Ishiyama Hongan-ji" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Takafuji · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A7%E5%9D%82%E6%9C%AC%E9%A1%98%E5%AF%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1533 CE – 1580 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ishiyama (Osaka)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shonyo, Kennyo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jodo Shinshu Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hongan-ji, Ishiyama Honganji</dd></dl></aside></div>

